## 3. Codebase Analysis & Strategic Guidance

The following analysis is based on my direct review of the current codebase. Use these notes and tips to guide your implementation.

### Relevant Existing Code
*   **File:** `pom.xml:1`
    *   **Summary:** Parent aggregator POM that currently lists only `modules/core-platform` (`pom.xml:13`) and centralizes build-wide properties/plugins—Quarkus 3.20.0 BOM + AWS SDK BOM (`pom.xml:17-52`), dependency stubs for Stripe/Resilience4j/MapStruct/Testcontainers (`pom.xml:53-112`), and plugin management wiring Quarkus, Spotless, Surefire, JaCoCo, and OWASP Dependency-Check into the `verify` phase (`pom.xml:116-356`). It already defines dev/test/prod/native profiles with the `quarkus.profile` property and native build toggles (`pom.xml:359-423`).
    *   **Recommendation:** Keep shared dependency versions inside `<dependencyManagement>` so downstream modules inherit them; if the Standard Kit lists a Quarkus extension or SDK that'"'s missing, add its version coordinate here before referencing it in "'`modules/core-platform`. Honor the existing JaCoCo exclusions and profile structure—when enabling GraalVM or additional CI hooks, extend the `native` profile (`pom.xml:410-423`) rather than inventing new ones, and ensure `mvn clean verify` still executes Spotless + JaCoCo as defined in the plugin executions.
*   **File:** `docs/quality/test_strategy.md:5`
    *   **Summary:** Documents the module inventory (currently parent + `modules/core-platform`), mandatory Maven profiles (`dev`/`test`/`prod`/`native`, `docs/quality/test_strategy.md:41-78`), Quarkus extension inventory aligned with the Standard Kit (`docs/quality/test_strategy.md:79-114`), coverage thresholds + exclusions, and the required `./mvnw clean verify` workflow along with an acceptance-criteria checklist for `I1.T1` (`docs/quality/test_strategy.md:237-243`).
    *   **Recommendation:** Update the Module Inventory and Quarkus Extension sections anytime you change the dependency mix or add modules so QA has a trustworthy reference; mirror any tweaks to Maven profiles or verification commands in the "Maven Build Verification" block (`docs/quality/test_strategy.md:218-235`) so this document remains the canonical pointer for how to run the build across `%dev/%test/%prod/%native`.
*   **File:** `docs/java-project-standards.adoc:362`
    *   **Summary:** Organization-wide standards describing mandatory tooling—Spotless config and formatter expectations (`docs/java-project-standards.adoc:362-430`), JaCoCo requirements (`docs/java-project-standards.adoc:462-520`), and Sonar quality gates (`docs/java-project-standards.adoc:524-566`).
    *   **Recommendation:** Use this file as your checklist when auditing the Maven build: confirm Spotless and JaCoCo configurations in `pom.xml` stay aligned with the snippets here, and record any justified deviations (e.g., Quarkus platform version) in plan notes/ADRs since this document treats the Standard Kit as frozen.

### Implementation Tips & Notes
*   **Tip:** Run `./mvnw clean verify` from the repository root to satisfy the acceptance criteria—this command triggers Spotless, JaCoCo, OWASP Dependency-Check, and both JVM/native verifications as documented in `docs/quality/test_strategy.md:218-235`.
*   **Tip:** When adding Quarkus extensions or SDKs for compliance, manage their versions via `<dependencyManagement>` (`pom.xml:34-114`) and keep module-specific declarations inside the module POM so that future modules inherit consistent versions automatically.
*   **Note:** Any change to Maven profile semantics (e.g., toggling SQL logs or enabling dev UI) must be reflected both in the `<profiles>` section (`pom.xml:359-423`) and in the profile documentation within `docs/quality/test_strategy.md:41-78`; this dual update is explicitly called out in the deliverables for `I1.T1`.
*   **Warning:** The Standard Kit requires GraalVM-native compatibility; avoid introducing dependencies that rely on unsupported reflection or runtime proxies without adding the necessary Quarkus native-image configuration, and document any unavoidable limitations in the test strategy so that CI maintainers know how to interpret native build failures.
